The table structure information is ({database name: {schema name: [table name]}}): 
{'bigquery-public-data': {'stackoverflow': ['posts_answers', 'users', 'posts_orphaned_tag_wiki', 'posts_tag_wiki', 'stackoverflow_posts', 'posts_questions', 'comments', 'posts_tag_wiki_excerpt', 'posts_wiki_placeholder', 'posts_privilege_wiki', 'post_history', 'badges', 'post_links', 'tags', 'votes', 'posts_moderator_nomination']}}
Begin Exploring Related Columns
Relevant columns and their usage  

bigquery-public-data.stackoverflow.posts_answers  
• owner_user_id : who wrote each answer – use to count answers per user  
• id            : primary key of each answer – can be counted  
• post_type_id  : (always 2 in this table) – identifies an “answer”  

bigquery-public-data.stackoverflow.users  
• id            : join key to posts_answers.owner_user_id  
• display_name  : name we finally need to show  
• reputation    : filter to “> 10” as required  

Typical conditions we will use  
• a.owner_user_id IS NOT NULL  
• u.reputation > 10  
• (p.post_type_id = 2 when we use the generic posts table)  

Sample‑to‑complex exploration queries
```sql
-- 1. Peek at a few raw answers
SELECT id, owner_user_id, score
FROM `bigquery-public-data.stackoverflow.posts_answers`
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Simple count of answers per user (unsorted sample)
SELECT owner_user_id, COUNT(*) AS answers_cnt
FROM `bigquery-public-data.stackoverflow.posts_answers`
GROUP BY owner_user_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Same count but sorted by the largest contributors
SELECT owner_user_id, COUNT(*) AS answers_cnt
FROM `bigquery-public-data.stackoverflow.posts_answers`
GROUP BY owner_user_id
ORDER BY answers_cnt DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Look at users whose reputation is already above 10
SELECT id, display_name, reputation
FROM `bigquery-public-data.stackoverflow.users`
WHERE reputation > 10
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Join answers to their authors (quick sample)
SELECT u.display_name, u.reputation, a.id AS answer_id
FROM `bigquery-public-data.stackoverflow.posts_answers` AS a
JOIN `bigquery-public-data.stackoverflow.users`        AS u
  ON a.owner_user_id = u.id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Answers per display name (no reputation filter yet)
SELECT u.display_name, COUNT(*) AS answers_cnt
FROM `bigquery-public-data.stackoverflow.posts_answers` AS a
JOIN `bigquery-public-data.stackoverflow.users`        AS u
  ON a.owner_user_id = u.id
GROUP BY u.display_name
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Answers per user, requiring reputation > 10
SELECT u.display_name,
       u.reputation,
       COUNT(*)        AS answers_cnt
FROM `bigquery-public-data.stackoverflow.posts_answers` AS a
JOIN `bigquery-public-data.stackoverflow.users`        AS u
  ON a.owner_user_id = u.id
WHERE u.reputation > 10
GROUP BY u.display_name, u.reputation
ORDER BY answers_cnt DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Do the same using the all‑posts table, explicitly checking post_type_id = 2
SELECT u.display_name, COUNT(*) AS answers_cnt
FROM `bigquery-public-data.stackoverflow.stackoverflow_posts` AS p
JOIN `bigquery-public-data.stackoverflow.users`              AS u
  ON p.owner_user_id = u.id
WHERE p.post_type_id = 2              -- answers
  AND u.reputation  > 10
GROUP BY u.display_name
ORDER BY answers_cnt DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Re‑run on posts_answers, excluding null user ids
SELECT u.display_name, COUNT(*) AS answers_cnt
FROM `bigquery-public-data.stackoverflow.posts_answers` AS a
JOIN `bigquery-public-data.stackoverflow.users`        AS u
  ON a.owner_user_id = u.id
WHERE a.owner_user_id IS NOT NULL
  AND u.reputation     > 10
GROUP BY u.display_name
ORDER BY answers_cnt DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. Final answer: who answered the most questions (reputation > 10)
SELECT u.display_name
FROM `bigquery-public-data.stackoverflow.posts_answers` AS a
JOIN `bigquery-public-data.stackoverflow.users`        AS u
  ON a.owner_user_id = u.id
WHERE u.reputation > 10
GROUP BY u.display_name
ORDER BY COUNT(*) DESC
LIMIT 20;         -- top row is the single highest‑answer user
```
